<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pithekoussai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pithekoussai was an early Greek settlement on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a>, founded in the first half of the 8th century BCE by colonists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and Eretria in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>. It was the earliest lasting Greek foundation in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and a trading post for metals; finds include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a>'s Cup, bearing one of the oldest Greek inscriptions. Its settlers later founded Cumae on the mainland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pithekoussai.jpg" alt="Pithekoussai" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Retaggio · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ischia_da_procida.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
